.TH BGREP 1 "bgrep 0.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bgrep \- grep for binary data
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bgrep
.RI [ OPTION ]\|.\|.\|.
.I PATTERN
.RI [ FILE ]\|.\|.\|.
.br
.B bgrep
.RI [ OPTION ]\|.\|.\|.
.RB { \-e " \fIPATTERN\fR | " \-f " \fIFILE\fR }\|.\|.\|."
.RI [ FILE ]\|.\|.\|.
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bgrep
searches the named input
.IR FILE s
(or standard input if none are given, or if a
.I FILE
is
.BR \- )
for byte sequences that match
.IR PATTERN ,
and prints the offset and contents of each match.
Where
.BR grep (1)
works on lines of text,
.B bgrep
works on arbitrary binary data: a known header, magic number or record
layout can be located inside a file by describing it as hexadecimal.
.PP
Matches never overlap; after a match the search resumes just past its end.
When several patterns match at the same position, the one given first wins.
.PP
The command-line interface and this page borrow heavily from
.BR grep (1)
so that the two feel alike.
.
.SH "PATTERN SYNTAX"
A pattern is written in the hexadecimal notation of the bytes to find.
.SS "Default (\-F): hex digits and wildcards"
.PP
The pattern is a string of hexadecimal digits (\fB0\-9a\-fA\-F\fP) in which
a period
.RB ( . )
stands for any single hex digit (a \(lqnibble\(rq).
White space is ignored.
The number of digits (including wildcards) must be even.
.PP
.nf
.RS
68 65 6c 6c 6f      the bytes "hello"
de ad be ef         DEADBEEF
ff d8 ff ..         a JPEG start marker followed by any byte
4. 50               a byte 0x40\-0x4f followed by 0x50
.RE
.fi
.SS "Regular expressions (\-G, \-E)"
With
.B \-G
or
.B \-E
the pattern is a POSIX basic or extended regular expression
.RB ( regex (7))
applied to the data rendered as continuous lowercase hexadecimal text
(two characters per byte, no separators).
White space in the pattern is removed and matching is case-insensitive.
The period still matches one nibble, so
.B ..
matches one byte, and the full power of regular expressions is available:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\-E '504b(0304|0506)'           ZIP local header or end record
\-E 'ff(d8|d9)'                 JPEG start or end marker
\-E '00{8,}'                    eight or more zero bytes
\-E '^7f454c46'                 an ELF header at the very start
.RE
.fi
.PP
Matches must start and end on a byte boundary (an even character
position); a match that does not is skipped and the search continues one
character further on.  For this reason a pattern such as
.B 8656
does not match inside
.BR 6865\^6c6c ,
unless
.B \-\^\-half\-byte
is given.
.PP
Regex mode reads each input completely into memory, using about three times
its size.  The default mode streams its input in constant memory.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Pattern selection"
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\^\-fixed
Interpret patterns as hex digits and wildcards (the default).
.TP
.BR \-G ", " \-\^\-basic\-regexp
Interpret patterns as POSIX basic regular expressions.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\^\-extended\-regexp
Interpret patterns as POSIX extended regular expressions.
.TP
.BI \-e " PATTERN" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-regexp=" PATTERN
Use
.I PATTERN
as a pattern.  May be given multiple times.  When
.B \-e
or
.B \-f
is used, no positional
.I PATTERN
is expected.
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-file=" FILE
Read patterns from
.IR FILE ,
one per line; blank lines are ignored.  Use
.B \-
for standard input.  Unlike earlier versions, \fB\-f\fP does
.I not
name a file to search; list files as ordinary arguments.
.SS "General output control"
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\^\-count
Print only the number of matches in each file.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\^\-files\-with\-matches
Print only the names of files that contain a match.  Scanning of a file
stops at its first match.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\^\-files\-without\-match
Print only the names of files that contain no match.
.TP
.BI \-m " NUM" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-max\-count=" NUM
Stop reading a file after
.I NUM
matches.  0 (the default) means no limit.  Decimal, hexadecimal
(leading \fB0x\fP) and octal (leading \fB0\fP) are accepted.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\^\-quiet ", " \-\^\-silent
Write nothing; exit immediately with status 0 as soon as any match is
found, even if an error occurred earlier.
.SS "Output line prefix control"
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\^\-byte\-offset
Print the offset of each match before it (the default).  The base is
described under \fBNumber formats\fP below.
.TP
.B \-\^\-no\-byte\-offset
Do not print the offset.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\^\-with\-filename
Prefix each output line with the file name.  This is the default when
more than one file is searched.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\^\-no\-filename
Never prefix output lines with the file name.
.TP
.BI \-\^\-label= LABEL
Use
.I LABEL
instead of \(lq(stdin)\(rq as the name of standard input.
.SS "Input control"
.TP
.BI \-s " OFFSET" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-starting\-offset=" OFFSET
Begin searching at byte
.I OFFSET
(default 0).  Reported offsets are still relative to the start of the file.
Seekable files are seeked; pipes are read and discarded.
.TP
.B \-\^\-half\-byte
Match on half-byte (nibble) boundaries.  Patterns may then have an odd number
of digits, and a match may begin on either half of a byte.  A match starting
on the low half of byte 2 has offset \fB2.5\fP (\(lq two and a half bytes\(rq),
and is printed as the matched nibbles regrouped from the match start in
pairs, so finding \fB8656\fP inside \fB68 65 6c\fP prints \fB86 56\fP.
Combine with \fB\-\^\-last\-byte\fP to report the position of the last matched
nibble.  Cannot be combined with context bytes, \fB\-I\fP, \fB\-\^\-ascii\fP or
the grouping options.
.SS "Offset reporting"
In the case of several of these options, the last one given wins.
.TP
.B \-\^\-first\-byte
Report the offset of the first byte of the match (default).
.TP
.B \-\^\-first\-byte\-with\-prefix
Report the offset of the first byte shown, including leading context bytes.
.TP
.B \-\^\-last\-byte
Report the offset of the last byte of the match.
.TP
.B \-\^\-last\-byte\-with\-suffix
Report the offset of the last byte shown, including trailing context bytes.
.SS "Number formats"
Numeric option arguments (\fB\-m\fP, \fB\-s\fP, \fB\-A\fP, \fB\-B\fP, \fB\-P\fP,
\fB\-S\fP, \fB\-O\fP, \fB\-I\fP, \fB\-g\fP, \fB\-p\fP) are interpreted in the
base chosen here, wherever these options appear on the command line.
.TP
.B \-\^\-auto\-mode
Arguments are decimal unless prefixed with \fB0x\fP (hexadecimal) or
\fB0\fP (octal).  Offsets are printed in hexadecimal and counts (\fB\-c\fP)
in decimal.  This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\^\-hex\-mode
Arguments are hexadecimal (no \fB0x\fP needed) and offsets and counts are
printed in hexadecimal.
.TP
.B \-\^\-oct\-mode
Arguments are octal; offsets and counts are printed in octal.
.TP
.B \-\^\-dec\-mode
Arguments are decimal; offsets and counts are printed in decimal.
.TP
.B \-\^\-hex\-prefix\fR, \fP\-\^\-no\-hex\-prefix
Print (or do not print, the default) \fB0x\fP before hexadecimal offsets.
.TP
.B \-\^\-hex\-suffix\fR, \fP\-\^\-no\-hex\-suffix
Print (or do not print, the default) \fBh\fP after hexadecimal offsets.
.TP
.BR \-p [\fIDIGITS\fP] ", " \-\^\-pad\-output [ =\fIDIGITS\fP ]
Pad offsets with leading zeros: to \fIDIGITS\fP digits if given, otherwise to
the width of the largest offset in the file (for pipes, 4 GiB is assumed).
The argument must be attached (\fB\-p4\fP, not \fB\-p 4\fP).
.TP
.B \-\^\-no\-pad\-output
Do not pad offsets (default).
.SS "Context bytes"
Context bytes are printed around the match, set off with \(lq | \(rq, and are
clipped at the start and end of the file.  They are not matched against, and
neighbouring matches may print the same context.  At most 1048576 bytes of
context in either direction are supported.
.TP
.BI \-B " NUM" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-before\-bytes=" NUM
Also print
.I NUM
bytes before the match.
.TP
.BI \-A " NUM" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-after\-bytes=" NUM
Also print
.I NUM
bytes after the match.
.TP
.BI \-P " K" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-prefix\-num\-bytes=" K
Print as many bytes before the match as the value of the \fIK\fPth byte
(1-based) of the matched data.  This follows length fields in the data, whose
size is not known when the pattern is written.  Added to \fB\-B\fP.  Ignored
if the match has fewer than \fIK\fP bytes.
.TP
.BI \-S " K" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-suffix\-num\-bytes=" K
Likewise for bytes after the match.  Added to \fB\-A\fP.
.TP
.BI \-O " \fR[\fP+|\-\fR]\fP" NUM "\fR, \fP\-\^\-off\-by=" [+|\-]NUM
Adjust the counts obtained with \fB\-P\fP and \fB\-S\fP by \fINUM\fP, to allow
for a length field that includes (or excludes) its own header.  \fB+\fP adds;
\fB\-\fP subtracts; with no sign the adjustment is negative.  Counts never go
below zero.
.SS "Byte display and transforms"
.TP
.B \-\^\-ascii
Append a hex-editor style rendering of everything printed, with
non-printable bytes shown as \fB.\fP.
.TP
.B \-\^\-no\-ascii
Do not (default).
.TP
.BI \-g " NUM" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-group\-size=" NUM
Print bytes in groups of
.I NUM
bytes, each group joined together and groups separated by a space (as
.BR xxd (1)
does).  Default 1.  Maximum 4096.  The match, leading context and trailing
context are each grouped separately.
.TP
.B \-\^\-big\-endian\-group
Show the bytes of each group in stream order (default).
.TP
.B \-\^\-little\-endian\-group
Show the bytes of each group last byte first, so a group reads as a
little-endian number.
.TP
.B \-\^\-byte\-reverse\-group
Reverse the order of the bytes shown in each group (applied after the
endian option, so \fB\-\^\-little\-endian\-group \-\^\-byte\-reverse\-group\fP
cancel out).
.TP
.B \-\^\-bit\-reverse\-group
Reverse the order of all the bits in each group (the byte order reverses
and the bits inside each byte reverse).
.TP
.BI \-I " K\fR[\fP,K...\fR]\fP" "\fR, \fP\-\^\-invert\-byte=" K
Print the \fIK\fPth byte(s) (1-based) of the match with all bits inverted.
May be given more than once.  The ASCII column shows the original bytes.
Positions beyond the match are ignored.
.SS "Miscellaneous"
.TP
.B \-\^\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\^\-version
Print the version and license and exit.
.SS "Planned options (not yet implemented)"
.TP
.BI \-\^\-input\-byte= BITS
.TP
.BI \-\^\-output\-byte= BITS
Work with bytes of other than 8 bits.  Recognized but currently exit with
status 2 and the message \(lqnot yet implemented\(rq.
.
.SH "OUTPUT FORMAT"
Each match is printed on its own line as
.PP
.RS
[\fIFILE\fP\fB:\fP][\fIOFFSET\fP\fB:\fP][\fIBEFORE\fP \fB|\fP] \fIBB BB BB\fP... [\fB|\fP \fIAFTER\fP]  [\fB|\fIascii\fB|\fR]
.RE
.PP
where
.I OFFSET
is the (by default hexadecimal) offset of the first matched byte and the
bytes follow as two-digit hexadecimal values separated by spaces.  The
optional parts appear according to \fB\-H\fP, \fB\-\^\-no\-byte\-offset\fP,
the context options and \fB\-\^\-ascii\fP.
.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
At least one match was found (with \fB\-L\fP: at least one file was listed).
.TP
.B 1
No match was found.
.TP
.B 2
An error occurred (bad option or pattern, unreadable file, ...).  This
takes precedence over 0 and 1, except that \fB\-q\fP exits 0 on a match.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find the string \(lqhello\(rq followed by a NUL byte:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \(aq68 65 6c 6c 6f 00\(aq data.bin
.RE
.PP
List the files in a tree that begin with a PNG signature:
.PP
.RS
.B find . \-type f \-exec bgrep \-lE \(aq^89504e470d0a1a0a\(aq {} +
.RE
.PP
Count ELF headers in a disk image:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-c \(aq7f 45 4c 46\(aq disk.img
.RE
.PP
A record with a length byte at position 3 (\fBAB\fP, length): show the
record header and the bytes the length field announces:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-S 3 \(aq41 42 ..\(aq records.bin
.RE
.PP
The same, where the length counts its own two header bytes (so
subtract 2 from it) and a hex-editor view is wanted:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-\-ascii \-S 3 \-O 2 \(aq41 42 ..\(aq records.bin
.RE
.PP
Show 16 bytes of context on each side of a match, with decimal offsets
padded to the file size:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-\-dec\-mode \-p \-B 16 \-A 16 \(aqde ad be ef\(aq core.dump
.RE
.PP
View 32-bit little-endian words around a pattern:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-g 4 \-\-little\-endian\-group \-A 12 \(aq7f 45 4c 46\(aq a.out
.RE
.PP
Find a bit pattern that straddles a byte boundary (the nibbles
.B 8 6 5
inside \(lq68 65\(rq):
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-\-half\-byte 865 data.bin
.RE
.PP
Search a pipe, showing only the bytes:
.PP
.RS
.B dd if=/dev/sda bs=1M count=64 | bgrep \-\-no\-byte\-offset \(aqff d8 ff ..\(aq
.RE
.PP
Several patterns, from a file:
.PP
.RS
.B bgrep \-f magic.txt firmware.bin
.RE
.
.SH NOTES
.B bgrep
was originally written to examine data from a consumer EEG device.
.
.SH BUGS
In regex mode, if the leftmost-longest match at some position ends on a
half byte, a shorter byte-aligned match at the same position is not
considered; the search moves on instead.
.PP
